Androgenetic Alopecia
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Progressive symmetrical hair thinning, Bitemporal recession/Vertex balding, Follicular miniaturization

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-005
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, painful weeping scalp reaction following application of an unverified topical hair-growth solution.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute onset of severe, burning scalp pain, bright red erythema, and weeping fluid from the scalp following the use of a newly purchased topical treatment. Urgent assessment was necessary to evaluate for acute chemical burns or severe allergic contact dermatitis, initiate immediate anti-inflammatory treatment, and prevent secondary infection or permanent follicular scarring before the next available daytime appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My whole head is burning and leaking yellow fluid. I put this new hair growth serum on a few hours ago and now I'm in absolute agony."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a 5-year history of progressive symmetrical thinning of scalp hair, with bitemporal recession of the anterior hairline and vertex balding creating an M-shaped pattern. In an attempt to treat this, the patient applied a highly concentrated, unregulated topical solution purchased online 4 hours ago. Within 60 minutes, the patient developed intense burning, stinging, and weeping over the application sites. The baseline androgenetic alopecia typically exhibits a complete absence of scalp inflammation, scarring, or significant scaling; therefore, this acute, painful, exudative presentation represents a sudden, severe complication requiring immediate intervention.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Use of unregulated internet-purchased topical compounds.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, pacing, holding an ice pack to the head.
Physical Exam: The scalp demonstrates classic signs of androgenetic alopecia with bitemporal recession and miniaturization of hair follicles resulting in fine, short vellus hairs over the vertex. Superimposed on this, the entire vertex and frontal scalp exhibit striking, sharply demarcated, bright red erythema corresponding exactly to the area of product application. The skin is edematous, intensely tender to light touch, with multiple focal areas of serous weeping and micro-vesiculation. No purulent discharge. No facial or airway swelling.
Environmental Observations: The offending product is an unlabeled glass dropper bottle ordered internationally.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No prescribed medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Irritant/Allergic Contact Dermatitis superimposed on Androgenetic Alopecia - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Chemical Burn (Full thickness) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact epidermis, presence of weeping vesicles rather than eschar or white coagulation of the skin.

Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Rapid onset precisely following product application, bilateral symmetry matching application area, and lack of fever.

Seborrheic Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, painful, weeping, and vesicular nature of the presentation, which is far too severe and rapid for seborrheic dermatitis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly painful, weeping scalp dermatitis directly following the application of an unregulated topical compound for baseline androgenetic alopecia. Triage accurately flagged the acute burning and weeping as a potential severe chemical injury or anaphylactic precursor. Urgent after-hours clinical assessment was mandatory to rule out airway involvement, assess the depth of the chemical injury, ensure immediate decontamination of the scalp, and prescribe potent topical corticosteroids and systemic antihistamines. Failing to provide immediate intervention would result in prolonged severe pain, risk of secondary bacterial superinfection of the weeping skin, and potential permanent scarring alopecia replacing the benign androgenetic pattern.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Supervised immediate gentle washing of the scalp with lukewarm water to remove residual product. Prescribed topical Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% lotion to be applied twice daily to dry scalp. Prescribed oral Loratadine 10mg daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: By washing your scalp immediately with cool water, you have successfully removed the chemical irritant, which will stop further damage to your hair follicles. By strictly avoiding any unregulated online hair products in the future, you will protect your scalp from severe reactions and preserve the hair you currently have.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: GP review in 2-3 days to evaluate the resolution of the dermatitis and discuss safe, TGA-approved options for hair loss.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you develop any swelling around your eyes, lips, or throat, experience difficulty breathing, or if the clear weeping turns into thick, foul-smelling yellow pus, you must present to the Emergency Department immediately or call 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe reaction is isolated to the skin based on current findings. Allergic reactions can occasionally worsen over the first 24 hours.
Clinical Course: The burning and weeping should significantly decrease within 24-48 hours with the steroid lotion. Your baseline male pattern hair loss will remain unchanged once the skin heals.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor closely for any swelling moving down your face towards your airways. Seek immediate emergency care if this occurs.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The steroid lotion will rapidly shut down the intense immune reaction and inflammation caused by the chemical, stopping the weeping and pain. The antihistamine will control the underlying allergic response.
Actionable Steps: Apply a thin layer of the steroid lotion only to the red areas. Throw away the unverified product immediately to prevent accidental re-exposure.

